{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"Treat each `Iff` as an equivalence between two independently valid facts, so neither direction needs to use its hypothesis.\n\n- For the first `Iff`, closed sets are stable under specialization: if `x ⤳ y` and `x ∈ s`, then `y ∈ s`, contradicting `y ∉ s`. Conversely, an open embedding is inducing and has open range; open sets are stable under generalization, yielding the standard `IsOpenEmbedding.generalizingMap` fact.\n- For the second `Iff`, use the neighborhood-filter description of the separation quotient: `mk` preserves the relevant neighborhood filter, and `lift f hf ∘ mk = f`. Hence continuity of the lift at `mk x` is equivalent to continuity of `f` at `x`. The assumption `hf` is precisely what makes the lift well-defined. Finally, `Inseparable x y` means `nhds x = nhds y` (equivalently, specialization in both directions), so symmetry is immediate.\n\nThus the proof can be organized by splitting the outer conjunction and both equivalences, then discharging the four universal goals with the corresponding facts `IsClosed.not_specializes`, `IsOpenEmbedding.generalizingMap`, `SeparationQuotient.continuousAt_lift`, and `Inseparable.symm`. These are documented together in [Mathlib.Topology.Inseparable](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/Inseparable.html)."}}
